  "problem": "$T$ is a bijection $\\,\u27fa\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,\u27fa\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,\u27fa\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,\u27fa\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$",
